WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.901
-You mentioned your son.

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.269
We've talked about, in the past,

00:00:03.269 --> 00:00:06.439
you working with your daughter,
who is also an actor.

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.908
You have two other kids.

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:12.178
How do they -- Do they respond
well to your work?

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.413
I know their ages kind of
run the gamut,

00:00:14.413 --> 00:00:17.950
but do they like watching you
on screen?

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:21.420
-You know, I don't know what
other people's experiences are,

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.623
but mine is that my kids
really don't care

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:27.326
about my professional life
at all.

00:00:27.326 --> 00:00:28.728
They're not interested.

00:00:28.728 --> 00:00:32.365
Me being in a Marvel movie,
to them,

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.433
simply ruins a Marvel movie.
-Got it.

00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:38.237
-They're very excited about all
Marvel movies except this one.

00:00:38.237 --> 00:00:41.607
I mean, you know, they're glad
I might get a couple

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:42.975
free T-shirts out of it,

00:00:42.975 --> 00:00:46.879
but that's as far as
the fun meter goes.

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:50.650
-I mean, you have a nice working
relationship with your wife.

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.551
You guys, you know,
you produce things together.

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:54.020
Does that matter at all?

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.656
Do they think that because Mom
and Dad do something together

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.793
that it makes show business
worse or better, do you think?

00:01:01.927 --> 00:01:03.396
-I think better.
-Yeah.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.564
-I think there's
a different kind of --

00:01:05.564 --> 00:01:08.568
there's a certain kind of
parenting you do

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.503
by teaching them
to play baseball,

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:13.573
teaching them to drive, playing
with them and all of that,

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:18.043
and there's another kind of
parenting you do via leadership

00:01:18.043 --> 00:01:21.447
and showing your kids
that you care about each other

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:24.083
and you care about each other's
work and seeing Mom

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.953
working on something that she's
incredibly passionate about.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.655
I mean, how can you tell your
kids to follow their dreams

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:31.691
if you don't follow yours,
right?

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.560
It's hollow words.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:37.764
And I think seeing the love

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.265
we have for each other
and [bleep] --

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:42.034
You know, my great passion
in my life has been my work.

00:01:42.034 --> 00:01:44.003
You know, I love acting,

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:47.673
and for me not to be able to
share that with my wife is hard.

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:51.944
And her getting into producing,
it's this whole new thing for me

00:01:51.944 --> 00:01:53.812
because it's the first time
that there's --

00:01:53.812 --> 00:01:56.949
it sounds a little corny,
but there's love there.

00:01:56.949 --> 00:01:59.318
You know, when we did
"The Good Lord Bird" together

00:01:59.318 --> 00:02:01.019
or "Blaze," some of the other
things we have done,

00:02:01.019 --> 00:02:04.390
they're all rooted deeply
in things that we as a family

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.660
care about and things
that we want to put forward.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.630
And so the kids feel that.

00:02:10.630 --> 00:02:14.000
They feel that we're not...

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:16.135
you know, we're not just
trying to make a buck.

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:18.671
You know, we do obviously want
to pay our bills

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.474
and everything like that,
but we also want to achieve

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.376
whatever our little dreams are,
you know?

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:27.847
And I think -- I don't know what
they would say,

00:02:27.847 --> 00:02:30.283
but I hope that
when they're grown,

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:34.019
it'll have some kind of impact
to watch us working together,

00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:35.588
treating each other
with respect,

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:38.357
trying to help each other
fulfill our dreams.

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:40.760
I mean, I know it sounds so
corny when you say it out loud,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.132
but it works for us or at least
it has up until this minute.

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.634
Maybe after this interview,
it'll go badly.

00:02:48.634 --> 00:02:51.203
-You talk about the importance
of, you know,

00:02:51.203 --> 00:02:53.872
following your dreams and
the fact that you started this

00:02:53.872 --> 00:02:55.941
at a young age, and obviously,
you had a passion for it.

00:02:55.941 --> 00:02:58.678
Were your parents supportive
early on when you basically

00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:01.580
said to them, "This is the path
I want to take"?

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:04.717
-Well, nobody in my family
had ever been in the arts,

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:09.388
so it seemed a completely
foreign idea.

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:12.291
You know, lots of kids say
they want to be actors.

00:03:12.291 --> 00:03:15.528
I think that my father was
absolutely petrified

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.363
that I would just be broke,
I mean,

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.097
or turn into
some bohemian madman,

00:03:19.097 --> 00:03:22.067
which you did call me
David Koresh when it started.

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:24.037
[ Laughter ]
So I don't know.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.504
Maybe my father's dreams --

00:03:25.504 --> 00:03:27.774
I mean, his nightmares
were justified.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:32.912
They instilled in me that
if I was going to do it,

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:35.181
I had to take it
incredibly seriously,

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.383
and that was of value to me.

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:42.455
Or maybe their concern about it
forced me into a situation

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:45.991
of trying to prove it was
a worthwhile profession.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:48.027
The reasons why I act now

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.095
are different than when
I acted as a kid.

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:53.633
When I was a kid, I do think
it was about, you know,

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.835
those crass things,
wanting people to like you,

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:58.204
wanting to be noticed,
wanting to be different.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:02.508
And as you get older, you start
to see what can be accomplished

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:05.044
through the arts
and you start to believe in it,

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:07.347
and so I think they're for it.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.315
Now, my father has always --

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.818
he was initially very worried
for me, and since then,

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:14.020
he's been wildly supportive,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:17.356
and my mother is just always
negative, you know.

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:18.992
I mean, that's just her.

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:22.294
You know, basically I have to --
she's the first one to tell me

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:24.664
I'm not quite as good as
I want to be.

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.165
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:29.035
-Does she do this on sort of
a project-by-project level?

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:31.003
Is it, you know, after
she watches something like

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:33.539
"Good Lord Bird," is she like,
"Here's my thoughts on this"?

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:37.777
-Yeah, she watched
"The Good Lord Bird"

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:42.582
and she's like, "You know,
I really like John Brown,

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:45.217
but I don't think
you're doing him justice.

00:04:45.217 --> 00:04:51.724
I mean, I think you're missing
an essential intelligence level

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.959
that he definitely --
Have you read his letters?"

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:57.096
I'm like, "Mom, I played the guy
for a year!

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:59.332
I read his letters!"
Like, "Give me a break."

00:04:59.332 --> 00:05:01.867
And I can't make her happy.

00:05:01.867 --> 00:05:04.436
-The really heartbreaking thing
about that is

00:05:04.436 --> 00:05:06.772
giving a note on something
that's already done.

00:05:06.772 --> 00:05:08.975
Like, "I just feel like
you're almost there."

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:10.376
[ Laughter ]

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.078
-You know what my mother
did to me the other day?

00:05:12.078 --> 00:05:15.181
Sent me something really bad
on the Internet about me

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:17.316
with the headline
"please ignore this."

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:22.021
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:24.891
-Oh, that's really good.
-I mean, like --

00:05:27.459 --> 00:05:30.363
-But your dad from the
beginning, because I will say

00:05:30.363 --> 00:05:32.698
I think -- I don't want to,
like, stereotype,

00:05:32.698 --> 00:05:35.534
but certainly my experience
has been my dad is sort of

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.570
the harder judge and my mom is
the one who sort of

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.972
hand-holds the whole way,
and again, it's a great team

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:42.508
because I feel like you do want
some of both,

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.677
but your dad has always been
just there for it?

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:46.445
-They inverted. No.

00:05:46.445 --> 00:05:49.415
In the beginning,
my father was very skeptical.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.850
I mean, incredibly skeptical.

00:05:51.850 --> 00:05:54.353
I mean, I think he said to me,
"What do you --

00:05:54.353 --> 00:05:56.388
You think you're
Clint Eastwood?"

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:59.825
I was like, "Dad, I don't have
to be Clint Eastwood."

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:01.927
I mean, it's like saying, like,
"I want to play basketball,

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:03.996
I want to be Michael Jordan."

00:06:03.996 --> 00:06:06.164
I'm not saying I'm going to be
Clint Eastwood.

00:06:06.164 --> 00:06:08.034
I'm just saying I want to do
this with my life.

00:06:08.034 --> 00:06:13.906
Once he observed my passion
and my enthusiasm --

00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:15.007
I just think he was worried

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:17.042
I wasn't going to be able
to pay my bills.

00:06:17.042 --> 00:06:19.545
-What was the first pay-my-bills
job where they thought,

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:23.081
"Okay, enough of these have
happened, he's gonna be okay"?

00:06:23.081 --> 00:06:25.151
-Well, for my father,

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.286
it wasn't until I was
in a successful Western.

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:29.988
I mean, "Magnificent Seven" --
I was 44 before my dad was like,

00:06:29.988 --> 00:06:32.758
"Alright, alright,
you're in 'Mag Seven.'

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:34.994
Alright, that was a good movie.

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:37.129
I saw that.
I think you're working."

00:06:37.129 --> 00:06:40.666
And my mother, strangely --
they really were the invert.

00:06:40.666 --> 00:06:46.305
When I was younger, she was
super supportive of the idea.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.207
-Yeah.
-Of going into the arts.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:51.043
She was this very -- You know,
when I first tried writing,

00:06:51.043 --> 00:06:54.646
she'd be like,
"You're no Chekhov, you know?

00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:57.249
I mean, it's okay,
but just so you know,

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:59.218
it's not as good
as it could be."

00:06:59.218 --> 00:07:04.957
I mean, that's -- I think she
thinks she's giving me drive.

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:06.992
And I don't know
if that's accurate.

00:07:06.992 --> 00:07:09.562
-Well, I want to say this
as your friend.

00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:14.100
I think you have both outpaced
Chekhov and Clint Eastwood.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.534
-Whoo!

00:07:15.534 --> 00:07:17.770
What about David Koresh though?
What about Koresh?

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.338
-I'm gonna wait
until I see "Moon Knight."

00:07:19.338 --> 00:07:21.707
-Okay, okay, okay, okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:23.175
-Alright, come back soon,
my friend.

00:07:23.175 --> 00:07:24.310
It's always great to see you.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:26.045
"Meadowlark" is available now.

